<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      For each object $X \in \calC $, let $F_ X: \calC \rightarrow \SSet ^{op}$ denote the functor represented by $X$, and let $S(X)$ denote the collection of all morphisms $f$ such that $F_ X(f)$ is an equivalence. Since $F_ X$ preserves small colimits, Remark <a href="/tag/02DF31" data-tag="02DF31">5.5.4.10</a> implies that $S(X)$ is strongly saturated. We now observe that $S$ is the intersection $\bigcap S(X)$, where $X$ ranges over the class of all $S_0$-local objects of $\calC $. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>